    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d679">

                <pb facs="0636" n="552" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg552"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 867.48/703</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the British Ambassador</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Spring Rice</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-05">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 5, 1918</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: I thank you for your
                    letter of December 26, with regard to relief measures in Palestine, occupied by
                    British forces, and have informed the Joint Distribution Committee and the
                    Provisional Committee for General Zionist Affairs, of the contents of your
                    letter.</p>
                <p>The Department telegraphed to the American Ambassador in London on December
                        12,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d679-f1"/> directing him to ascertain from the
                    British Government what total sums of relief money might be sent monthly into
                    Palestine, but has as yet received no reply thereto. The Department has however,
                    noted the statement in your letter that the amounts forwarded ought to be
                    limited and has conveyed this information to the War Trade Board which is
                    competent to issue licenses for such remittances.</p>
                <p>We shall gladly lend you our assistance so that the desires of the British
                    authorities may be duly heeded and the Department will be glad at any time to
                    receive further suggestions as to the desires of the British Government in this
                    connection.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
